---
import Layout from "../layouts/Layout.astro";
import QuickAnswer from "../components/QuickAnswer.astro";
import MoreInCategory from "../components/MoreInCategory.astro";
import { authorSchema, authorName } from "../data/authors";
import AuthorAvatar from "../components/AuthorAvatar.astro";
import QuickPicks from "../components/QuickPicks.astro";
import ProductCard from "../components/ProductCard.astro";
import PlanTheJob from "../components/PlanTheJob.astro";
import AuthorBio from "../components/AuthorBio.astro";
import { amazonLink } from "../data/affiliate";
import TrustBadge from "../components/TrustBadge.astro";
import SocialProof from "../components/SocialProof.astro";
import productImages from "../data/product-images.json";
import { pageDates } from "../data/dates";

const moreInCategory = [
  {
    "title": "Best Portable Power Stations",
    "url": "/best-portable-power-station/"
  },
  {
    "title": "Best Home Generators for Power Outages",
    "url": "/best-home-generator-for-power-outages/"
  },
  {
    "title": "Best Portable Power Station for Camping",
    "url": "/best-portable-power-station-for-camping/"
  },
  {
    "title": "Best UPS Battery Backup",
    "url": "/best-ups-battery-backup/"
  }
];
const dates = pageDates(Astro.url.pathname, { published: "2026-11-03", modified: "2026-11-03" });
const title = "Battery Backup for CPAP: 7 Best in 2026, Sized by Nights";
const description =
  "Seven CPAP battery backups, from airline-safe 99Wh packs to 1kWh power stations, with humidifier wattage, DC vs AC runtime math and FAA battery limits.";

const PRODUCTS = [
  {
    "id": "ecoflow-river-3-plus",
    "name": "EcoFlow River 3 Plus Portable Power Station",
    "category": "286Wh LFP, 600W AC, 12V car port, EPS mode",
    "badge": "Best Overall for CPAP",
    "amazonUrl": "",
    "pros": [
      "Roughly 286Wh of LFP covers one to two humidifier-free nights",
      "Fast-switching EPS mode suits a bedside UPS role",
      "12V car-style port for a CPAP maker's DC converter",
      "Add-on battery can roughly triple capacity later",
      "Light enough to carry to the bedroom or campsite"
    ],
    "cons": [
      "Heated humidifier plus heated tube can drain it in a single night",
      "Too heavy and too large to carry on a plane",
      "Add-on battery costs extra and adds weight"
    ],
    "bottomLine": "The right size for most CPAP users: a quiet bedside UPS at home that doubles as a one-to-two-night camping battery.",
    "description": "The River 3 Plus is the size class most CPAP users should start with. EcoFlow rates it at about 286 watt-hours of LFP storage with 600 watts of AC output, so the inverter has enormous headroom over any CPAP and its humidifier. EcoFlow also lists an EPS (emergency power supply) mode with a switchover it rates at under 10 milliseconds, which is what lets it sit between the wall and the machine and take over during an outage without waking you. By the runtime math in this guide, a machine drawing 25 watts without humidification gets roughly 9 to 10 hours from the battery through a DC converter, about one full night with margin; turn on a heated humidifier and heated tube and that can shrink to a few hours. EcoFlow sells an extra battery that clips on and raises capacity substantially, which turns it into a multi-night camping unit. It is not airline-legal, so frequent flyers need a dedicated pack as well."
  },
  {
    "id": "medistrom-pilot-24-lite",
    "name": "Medistrom Pilot-24 Lite CPAP Battery",
    "category": "99Wh, 24V and 12V DC, under airline 100Wh limit",
    "badge": "Best for Air Travel",
    "amazonUrl": "",
    "pros": [
      "Rated just under 100Wh, so no airline approval is needed",
      "DC output avoids inverter losses entirely",
      "Small enough to pack in a CPAP travel bag",
      "Model-specific cables for widely used ResMed and Philips machines",
      "USB port for a phone in the tent or hotel"
    ],
    "cons": [
      "A full night is realistic only at low draw with the humidifier off",
      "Requires the correct machine-specific cable, sold separately for some models",
      "Expensive per watt-hour compared with a power station"
    ],
    "bottomLine": "The battery to pack for flights and backpacking; measure your draw, and buy two if one will not cover a night.",
    "description": "Medistrom builds the Pilot-24 Lite specifically for CPAP machines, and its main selling point is a capacity rated just under the 100 watt-hour line that most airlines and the FAA treat as the no-paperwork limit for carry-on lithium batteries. It outputs DC directly, so there is no inverter wasting a slice of the battery, and it uses a cable matched to your machine model, so check the compatibility list for your exact CPAP before buying. How long it lasts depends heavily on your draw: at low pressures with humidification and heated tubing off, some machines sip little enough for a full night, while at an average of 25 watts the math works out to about three and a half hours, so measure before you trust it. Medistrom also makes a 12-volt version, the Pilot-12 Lite, for machines that run on 12V; buying the wrong one is the most common mistake. Two Pilot-24 Lite packs are still airline-legal as spares in most cases, which is the cleanest way to cover a two-night trip."
  },
  {
    "id": "jackery-explorer-300-plus",
    "name": "Jackery Explorer 300 Plus Portable Power Station",
    "category": "288Wh LFP, 300W AC, about 8 pounds",
    "badge": "Best Lightweight Station",
    "amazonUrl": "",
    "pros": [
      "About 288Wh of LFP in a compact, carry-handle body",
      "300W inverter is plenty for any CPAP and humidifier",
      "12V car-style output for a DC converter",
      "Simple controls with little to configure"
    ],
    "cons": [
      "Not expandable",
      "300W limit rules out most other bedside appliances",
      "Like any 288Wh station, it is too large to fly with"
    ],
    "bottomLine": "A light, uncomplicated one-to-two-night CPAP station for camping and short outages.",
    "description": "Jackery's Explorer 300 Plus is a small LFP station with about 288 watt-hours of storage and a 300-watt inverter, according to Jackery's published specs, in a body that weighs roughly 8 pounds. For a CPAP that is all the output you need, even with a heated humidifier running, and the capacity puts it in the same one-to-two-night class as the River 3 Plus. Its 12V car-style socket is the better way to power a CPAP: plug in your machine maker's DC converter or a compatible DC cable and skip the inverter losses entirely. It does not expand, so if you think you will want three or four nights off-grid, a larger station is the smarter buy. For a weekend camping trip where you charge it full at home and maybe top it up from a folding panel or the car, it is about as simple as a CPAP battery gets. Confirm any UPS or pass-through behavior on Jackery's current spec sheet before relying on it as a bedside backup."
  },
  {
    "id": "anker-solix-c300",
    "name": "Anker SOLIX C300 Portable Power Station",
    "category": "288Wh LFP, 300W AC, 140W USB-C",
    "badge": "Best for Mixed Devices",
    "amazonUrl": "",
    "pros": [
      "About 288Wh of LFP in a compact upright body",
      "Strong USB-C output for laptops and phones alongside the CPAP",
      "12V car-style output for a DC converter",
      "Recharges from USB-C as well as solar and car"
    ],
    "cons": [
      "300W AC is the ceiling",
      "Not expandable",
      "Too large to carry on a plane"
    ],
    "bottomLine": "A strong choice if the CPAP shares the battery with a laptop and phones on trips.",
    "description": "Anker's SOLIX C300 is another 288 watt-hour LFP station with a 300-watt AC inverter, and its strength is the USB-C side: Anker rates its top USB-C port at 140 watts, enough to charge most laptops at full speed while a CPAP runs from the 12V or AC output. That makes it a good fit for travel nurses, van campers and anyone whose overnight load is a CPAP plus a laptop and phone. It can also recharge over USB-C, which means a powerful laptop-style charger or a car USB-C adapter can top it up without the proprietary brick. Capacity is the same one-to-two-night class as the other small stations here, and the same caveats apply: humidifier on, expect a single night or less; humidifier off on a DC converter, expect a night with margin. Anker also sells DC-only variants of this family; if you want to plug the CPAP's standard power brick in, make sure the version you buy has AC outlets."
  },
  {
    "id": "jackery-explorer-1000-v2",
    "name": "Jackery Explorer 1000 v2 Portable Power Station",
    "category": "1,070Wh LFP, 1,500W AC, multi-night",
    "badge": "Best for Multi-Night Outages",
    "amazonUrl": "",
    "pros": [
      "About 1,070Wh covers several nights even with some humidification",
      "1,500W output also runs a fridge or fan in the day",
      "Light for its capacity at about 24 pounds",
      "Fast AC recharge between nights when power returns briefly"
    ],
    "cons": [
      "Overkill and heavy for CPAP-only camping",
      "Not expandable",
      "Costs several times as much as a 288Wh station"
    ],
    "bottomLine": "The pick when an outage could last days and the CPAP is only one of the things you need to keep running.",
    "description": "If your outages tend to last more than a night, or you want the CPAP battery to keep a phone, lamp and fan going too, step up to the kilowatt-hour class. Jackery rates the Explorer 1000 v2 at 1,070 watt-hours of LFP and 1,500 watts continuous with a 3,000-watt surge, in a body of roughly 24 pounds. For a CPAP alone that is multiple nights: a 30-watt draw through the 12V port should run somewhere around 30 hours of sleep by the efficiency math in this guide, and even with a heated humidifier pulling 60 watts you are looking at two to three nights. The output is high enough to run a refrigerator during the day, which matters in a long outage. The trade-off is that it is far more battery than a weekend camper needs, and at roughly three times the weight of the 288Wh units it is not something you casually carry to a tent site. Check Jackery's current listing for its UPS switchover specification."
  },
  {
    "id": "ecoflow-delta-3-plus",
    "name": "EcoFlow Delta 3 Plus Portable Power Station",
    "category": "1,024Wh LFP, 1,800W AC, 10ms EPS, expandable",
    "badge": "Best Bedside UPS for Long Outages",
    "amazonUrl": "",
    "pros": [
      "About 1kWh of LFP covers several CPAP nights",
      "EcoFlow-rated 10ms EPS switchover for seamless bedside backup",
      "Expandable with extra batteries",
      "1,800W output handles a fridge or space fan during the day",
      "Fast wall charging"
    ],
    "cons": [
      "Around 28 pounds; it lives at home, not in a tent",
      "More expensive than the Jackery 1000 v2 at many retailers",
      "Fan noise during fast charging in a bedroom"
    ],
    "bottomLine": "The best choice for a CPAP user who wants an always-connected bedside backup that can also carry a multi-day outage.",
    "description": "The Delta 3 Plus is the kilowatt-hour station to choose if the job is primarily an always-on bedside UPS. EcoFlow rates it at 1,024 watt-hours of LFP, 1,800 watts continuous, and an EPS switchover of 10 milliseconds, which is fast enough that a CPAP plugged into it should not notice the grid dropping out. Leave it plugged in at the bedside, plug the CPAP into it, and an outage at 3 a.m. becomes a non-event. Capacity covers several nights with the humidifier off and a couple with it on, and EcoFlow's expansion batteries can extend that if you live where outages last a week. During the day the 1,800-watt inverter can run a refrigerator, which makes it a genuine home-backup tool rather than a single-purpose CPAP battery. Two cautions: fast charging spins up the fan, so set a slower charge rate in the app if it sits in the bedroom, and it is too heavy to be a convenient camping companion."
  },
  {
    "id": "honda-eu2200i",
    "name": "Honda EU2200i Inverter Generator",
    "category": "2,200W starting, 1,800W running, gasoline, inverter",
    "badge": "Best Generator for CPAP",
    "amazonUrl": "",
    "pros": [
      "Inverter output is suitable for sensitive electronics",
      "Among the quietest gas generators in its class",
      "Runs a CPAP plus a fridge or window fan",
      "Recharges a power station during the day",
      "Long reputation for reliability and parts support"
    ],
    "cons": [
      "Must run outdoors, far from windows, never in a garage or tent",
      "Gas storage and maintenance",
      "Running it all night for a 30W load is wasteful and noisy for neighbors"
    ],
    "bottomLine": "The best generator for a CPAP household, used to recharge a battery by day rather than to run the machine all night.",
    "description": "If you are searching for the best generator for a CPAP machine, the honest answer is that a generator should usually charge the battery that runs the CPAP, not run the machine directly through the night. With that framing, the Honda EU2200i is the benchmark. Honda rates it at 2,200 starting and 1,800 running watts, and as an inverter generator its output is clean enough for electronics like a CPAP power supply and a power station's charger. It is quiet for a gas generator, but it is still a combustion engine that produces carbon monoxide: it must run outdoors, well away from doors, windows and vents, with CO alarms inside. The smart routine is to run it for a few hours in the afternoon to recharge a power station and the fridge, then shut it down and let the battery carry the CPAP silently overnight. Running a 1,800-watt engine all night for a 30-watt machine is a poor use of fuel."
  }
];

const schema = {
  "@context": "https://schema.org",
  "@graph": [
    {
      "@type": "BreadcrumbList",
      itemListElement: [
        {
          "@type": "ListItem",
          position: 1,
          name: "Home",
          item: "https://www.allmetalbrake.com/",
        },
        {
          "@type": "ListItem",
          position: 2,
          name: "Best Lists",
          item: "https://www.allmetalbrake.com/best-of/",
        },
        {
          "@type": "ListItem",
          position: 3,
          name: "Best Battery Backup for CPAP",
          item: "https://www.allmetalbrake.com/best-battery-backup-for-cpap/",
        },
      ],
    },
    {
      "@type": "FAQPage",
      mainEntity: [
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "What is the best battery backup for a CPAP?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "For most people, a roughly 300 watt-hour LFP power station like the EcoFlow River 3 Plus, used with a DC cable, is the best all-round choice. It covers about a night with the humidifier off, works as a bedside backup, and doubles as a camping battery. Frequent flyers should add a sub-100Wh dedicated pack like the Medistrom Pilot-24 Lite."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "How long will a battery run a CPAP?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "Divide the battery's watt-hours, times about 0.9 on DC or 0.85 on AC, by your machine's average watts. A 288Wh station on DC with a 25-watt draw gives roughly 10 hours. Turn on a heated humidifier and the same battery may give only about 4 hours. Measure your machine's real draw with a watt meter for an accurate answer."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "Can I run my CPAP from a power station's AC outlet?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "Yes. Every station here has a pure sine wave inverter that can run a CPAP's standard power brick. The trade-off is efficiency: the inverter wastes energy, so you get less runtime than with a DC converter from the 12V port. Use AC if your machine needs it for heated humidification, and confirm the setup with your CPAP manufacturer."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "What size CPAP battery can I take on a plane?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "Under FAA rules for passengers, spare lithium batteries go in carry-on only. Batteries of 100Wh or less are generally allowed without approval; 100 to 160Wh need airline approval and are usually limited to two; anything over 160Wh is prohibited. Check your airline's own medical-device policy too, since carriers can be stricter."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "What is the best generator for a CPAP machine?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "A quiet inverter generator such as the Honda EU2200i, used outdoors to recharge a power station during the day. Inverter output suits sensitive electronics. Running a generator all night just for a CPAP wastes fuel, disturbs neighbors and puts a carbon monoxide source near sleeping people, so let a battery run the machine overnight."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "Will a CPAP restart by itself after a power outage?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "It depends on the machine and settings. Some CPAPs resume therapy when power returns, others wait for you to press start, and many users only discover the answer during an outage. A power station with a fast UPS or EPS mode avoids the question by keeping the machine running through the outage. Check your manual or ask your DME supplier."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "Is a power station a medical device?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "No. The power stations in this guide are general-purpose consumer batteries and are not medically certified. They supply power to your CPAP the same way a wall outlet does. Your CPAP manufacturer lists approved power accessories, and your doctor and DME supplier should confirm any battery setup before you rely on it for therapy."
          }
        }
      ],
    },
    {
      "@type": "Article",
      headline: title,
      author: authorSchema(Astro.url.pathname),
      datePublished: dates.published,
      dateModified: dates.modified,
      publisher: { "@type": "Organization", name: "All Metal Brake" },
    },
    {
      "@type": "ItemList",
      name: title,
      numberOfItems: PRODUCTS.length,
      itemListElement: PRODUCTS.map((product, index) => ({
        "@type": "ListItem",
        position: index + 1,
        name: product.name,
        url: new URL(`#${product.id}`, Astro.url).toString(),
      })),
    },
    ...PRODUCTS.map((product) => ({
      "@type": "Product",
      name: product.name,
      description: product.description,
      brand: {
        "@type": "Brand",
        name: product.name.split(" ")[0],
      },
    })),
  ],
};
---

<Layout
  title={title}
  description={description}
  ogType="article"
  article={{
    publishedTime: dates.published,
    modifiedTime: dates.modified,
    author: authorName(Astro.url.pathname),
    section: "Power & Generators",
    tags: [
      "battery backup for cpap",
      "cpap battery",
      "portable cpap for camping",
      "best generator for cpap machine",
      "cpap battery for travel",
      "cpap ups backup"
    ],
  }}
  jsonLd={schema}
>
  <article class="relative overflow-hidden">
    <!-- Hero Section -->
    <header data-hero class="bg-[#F4F6F8] border-b border-gray-200">
      <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 pt-10 pb-8 lg:pt-14 lg:pb-10">
        <p class="flex flex-wrap items-center gap-x-2 gap-y-1 text-xs font-bold uppercase tracking-widest m-0 mb-4">
          <span class="text-[#D93A00]">Buyer's Guide</span>
          <span class="text-gray-300" aria-hidden="true">&bull;</span>
          <span class="text-gray-500">Updated {dates.display}</span>
        </p>
        <h1 class="text-[2.1rem] leading-[1.15] sm:text-5xl font-black tracking-tight text-gray-900 m-0">
          The Best Battery Backup for CPAP
        </h1>
        <QuickAnswer products={PRODUCTS} />
        <p class="text-lg lg:text-xl text-gray-600 leading-relaxed mt-5 mb-0">
          Seven batteries that keep a CPAP running through an outage, a camping trip or a flight, with the humidifier wattage and runtime math that decide which size you need.
        </p>
        <!-- Author & Meta -->
        <div class="flex items-center gap-3 mt-7">
          <AuthorAvatar size={40} />
          <div class="min-w-0">
            <p class="text-sm text-gray-600 m-0">Written by <a href="/authors/alex-rivers/" class="font-bold text-gray-900 no-underline hover:underline">Alex Rivers</a></p>
            <p class="text-xs text-gray-500 m-0">Home Improvement Editor &middot; Updated {dates.display}</p>
          </div>
          <SocialProof users="" />
        </div>
        <div class="flex items-center gap-4 mt-6">
          <TrustBadge />
          <span class="flex-1 h-px bg-gray-300"></span>
        </div>
        <p class="text-sm text-gray-600 leading-relaxed mt-3 mb-0">
          We judge every product on its spec sheet, its label and what owners report over time, not on the marketing. No paid placements, and commission never sets a ranking. <a href="/how-we-test/" class="font-bold text-gray-900 underline">Read how we pick</a>
        </p>
      </div>
    </header>

    <!-- Table of Contents -->
    <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 pt-8">
      <details open class="group bg-white border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl px-5 pt-4 pb-2">
        <summary class="flex items-center gap-3 pb-3 cursor-pointer list-none [&::-webkit-details-marker]:hidden">
          <svg class="w-5 h-5 text-gray-500" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" viewBox="0 0 24 24" aria-hidden="true"><path stroke-linecap="round" d="M9 6h11M9 12h11M9 18h11M4.5 6h.01M4.5 12h.01M4.5 18h.01" /></svg>
          <span class="font-bold text-gray-900">In This Guide</span>
          <span class="text-xs font-semibold text-gray-400">12 sections</span>
          <svg class="w-5 h-5 ml-auto text-gray-500 transition-transform group-open:rotate-180" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" viewBox="0 0 24 24" aria-hidden="true"><path stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" d="M6 15l6-6 6 6" /></svg>
        </summary>
        <nav class="grid grid-cols-1 sm:grid-cols-2 gap-x-6">
              <a href="#quick-answer" class="flex items-baseline gap-3 py-2.5 border-t border-gray-100 text-[15px] text-gray-700 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline"><span class="w-6 shrink-0 text-gray-400 font-semibold">1.</span><span>The Short Answer</span></a>
              <a href="#top-picks" class="flex items-baseline gap-3 py-2.5 border-t border-gray-100 text-[15px] text-gray-700 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline"><span class="w-6 shrink-0 text-gray-400 font-semibold">2.</span><span>The Seven Picks</span></a>
              <a href="#cpap-watts" class="flex items-baseline gap-3 py-2.5 border-t border-gray-100 text-[15px] text-gray-700 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline"><span class="w-6 shrink-0 text-gray-400 font-semibold">3.</span><span>CPAP Watts</span></a>
              <a href="#dc-vs-ac" class="flex items-baseline gap-3 py-2.5 border-t border-gray-100 text-[15px] text-gray-700 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline"><span class="w-6 shrink-0 text-gray-400 font-semibold">4.</span><span>DC Cable vs AC</span></a>
              <a href="#runtime-math" class="flex items-baseline gap-3 py-2.5 border-t border-gray-100 text-[15px] text-gray-700 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline"><span class="w-6 shrink-0 text-gray-400 font-semibold">5.</span><span>Nights of Runtime</span></a>
              <a href="#airline" class="flex items-baseline gap-3 py-2.5 border-t border-gray-100 text-[15px] text-gray-700 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline"><span class="w-6 shrink-0 text-gray-400 font-semibold">6.</span><span>Flying With a Battery</span></a>
              <a href="#camping" class="flex items-baseline gap-3 py-2.5 border-t border-gray-100 text-[15px] text-gray-700 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline"><span class="w-6 shrink-0 text-gray-400 font-semibold">7.</span><span>Camping With a CPAP</span></a>
              <a href="#ups-mode" class="flex items-baseline gap-3 py-2.5 border-t border-gray-100 text-[15px] text-gray-700 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline"><span class="w-6 shrink-0 text-gray-400 font-semibold">8.</span><span>UPS Mode</span></a>
              <a href="#comparison" class="flex items-baseline gap-3 py-2.5 border-t border-gray-100 text-[15px] text-gray-700 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline"><span class="w-6 shrink-0 text-gray-400 font-semibold">9.</span><span>Comparison Table</span></a>
              <a href="#which-to-buy" class="flex items-baseline gap-3 py-2.5 border-t border-gray-100 text-[15px] text-gray-700 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline"><span class="w-6 shrink-0 text-gray-400 font-semibold">10.</span><span>Which One to Buy</span></a>
              <a href="#mistakes" class="flex items-baseline gap-3 py-2.5 border-t border-gray-100 text-[15px] text-gray-700 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline"><span class="w-6 shrink-0 text-gray-400 font-semibold">11.</span><span>Common Mistakes</span></a>
              <a href="#faq" class="flex items-baseline gap-3 py-2.5 border-t border-gray-100 text-[15px] text-gray-700 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline"><span class="w-6 shrink-0 text-gray-400 font-semibold">12.</span><span>FAQ</span></a>
        </nav>
      </details>
    </div>

    <!-- Content -->
    <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 pb-16 lg:pb-24">
      <div class="prose prose-lg prose-blue max-w-none text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">
        <p class="text-2xl font-medium text-gray-900 mb-8 italic">
          A CPAP barely sips power until you turn on the heated humidifier. Size the battery for the settings you will actually sleep with, and the right pick becomes obvious.
        </p>

        <QuickPicks products={PRODUCTS} />

        <div id="quick-answer" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            1. The Short Answer: What Size Battery Your CPAP Needs
          </h2>

          <p>
            A CPAP is one of the easiest medical devices to back up with a battery, and also one of the easiest to get wrong. The machine itself is frugal; the heated humidifier and heated tube are not. A battery that comfortably covers two nights with humidification off may not make it to dawn with everything on, and that difference is what decides whether you need a 99 watt-hour travel pack, a 288 watt-hour camping station or a kilowatt-hour box beside the bed.
          </p>

          <p>
            This guide covers both kinds of battery backup for CPAP users. Dedicated CPAP batteries like the Medistrom Pilot-24 Lite are small, airline-friendly and output DC straight to the machine. Portable power stations like the EcoFlow River 3 Plus and Jackery Explorer 1000 v2 are bigger lithium iron phosphate (LFP) batteries with AC outlets, 12V sockets and USB, and some of them can sit between the wall and the CPAP as an uninterruptible power supply. If you want the wider picture first, our <a href="/best-portable-power-station/">portable power station rankings</a> cover the general-purpose units these picks are drawn from.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#38BDF8] mb-3">THE SHORT ANSWER</p>
            <p class="text-xl font-bold text-white mb-3">For most people, a roughly 300Wh LFP station with a 12V DC cable is the right first CPAP battery.</p>
            <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed m-0">It covers one to two nights with the humidifier off, works as a bedside backup, and doubles as a camping battery. Fly often? Add a sub-100Wh dedicated pack. Long outages? Step up to a 1kWh station.</p>
          </div>

          <p>
            Before anything else: this is a buying guide, not medical advice. Your CPAP manufacturer publishes approved power accessories, and your durable medical equipment (DME) supplier and doctor should confirm that a given battery or DC cable is suitable for your machine and prescription. None of the power stations here is a medical device or medically certified, and none of them changes what your machine is approved to do. What they change is whether your therapy stops when the power does.
          </p>

          <p>
            The picks below are described from manufacturers' published specifications and the runtime math later in this guide, not from hands-on testing. Every runtime figure is an estimate. Your pressure setting, mask leak, humidifier level, room temperature and machine model all move the real number, which is why the most useful thing in this article is the method for calculating your own nights of runtime, not any single headline figure.
          </p>
