        <div id="cpap-watts" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            3. How Many Watts a CPAP Uses, With and Without the Humidifier
          </h2>

          <p>
            The number printed on your CPAP's power brick is not what it uses. That label is the maximum the supply can deliver, and on popular machines such as the ResMed AirSense series it is typically 90 watts. Actual draw is far lower most of the night. The blower motor at typical pressures uses a modest amount; what pushes consumption up is the heating element in the humidifier tub and, if you use one, the heated tube. Those heaters can easily double or triple the machine's average draw, especially in a cold bedroom.
          </p>

          <p>
            As a planning range, many modern CPAP machines average somewhere around 15 to 40 watts with humidification and heated tubing switched off, depending on pressure. With a heated humidifier and heated tube running, plan for roughly 50 to 90 watts, with the high end in cold rooms and at high humidity settings. BiPAP and auto-adjusting machines at high pressures sit toward the top of each range. These are broad bands for sizing, not a spec for your machine.
          </p>

