                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">480-800Wh+</td>
                  </tr>
                </tbody>
              </table>
            </div>
          </div>

          <p>
            The best way to get your real number is to measure it. A plug-in watt meter between the wall and the CPAP's power brick will show average watts and total watt-hours over a night, and those meters are inexpensive. Run one for two or three nights with your normal settings, then once with the humidifier off. The difference tells you exactly how much battery the humidifier costs you, which is the single most important number in this whole decision. Some machines also report usage data in their app, but a meter at the wall captures the power brick's own losses too.
          </p>

          <p>
            Most people who rely on a battery end up turning the humidifier down or off on battery nights. That is a comfort trade-off, not a requirement, and it is worth asking your doctor or sleep clinic whether a night or two without heated humidification is acceptable for you. If it is not, plan for a bigger battery rather than hoping a small one stretches. A battery that dies at 3 a.m. is worse than a heavier battery that makes it to morning.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="dc-vs-ac" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            4. DC Cable vs AC Inverter: The Free Runtime Most People Miss
          </h2>

          <p>
            Every portable power station stores energy as DC and has to convert it to 120-volt AC through an inverter to power a standard wall plug. Your CPAP's power brick then converts that AC back to the low-voltage DC the machine actually runs on. Each conversion wastes energy as heat, and the inverter also draws its own idle power just from being switched on. Together, running a CPAP on AC from a battery often loses something like 10 to 20 percent of the battery compared with powering it directly on DC.
          </p>

          <p>
            The fix is a DC converter or cable. ResMed, Philips and other manufacturers sell DC power accessories for their machines that plug into a 12V car-style socket, and battery makers like Medistrom sell model-specific cables for their packs. Plug that into the 12V output of a power station and the station's inverter never turns on. On a small 288Wh station, that saving alone can be the difference between one night and a night and a half.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#38BDF8] mb-3">WORTH KNOWING</p>
            <p class="text-xl font-bold text-white mb-3">DC power often disables or limits the heated humidifier.</p>
            <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed m-0">Some machines restrict humidifier heating when running from a DC converter or battery to conserve power. Check your manual. If you rely on heated humidification, you may have to use AC from the power station anyway, and size the battery for it.</p>
          </div>

          <p>
            Compatibility is where the details matter. ResMed AirSense machines run on a 24-volt supply, so they need a converter that steps up from 12V or a battery with a native 24V output, such as the Pilot-24 Lite. Some other machines run on 12V and use a different cable or a different battery model entirely. Buying the right battery with the wrong cable is the most common and most avoidable CPAP battery mistake. Take your machine's exact model name to the battery maker's compatibility chart, or ask your DME supplier, before ordering anything.
          </p>

          <p>
            If you do run on AC, use a station with a pure sine wave inverter, which every station on this list has, and plug the CPAP's own power brick in rather than a cheap universal adapter. Turn off any other AC loads you do not need so the inverter is not working harder than necessary, and switch the inverter off in the morning. An inverter left idling with nothing plugged in can quietly eat a meaningful slice of a small battery over a day.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="runtime-math" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            5. Nights of Runtime: The Math, Worked Out
          </h2>

          <p>
            Runtime math for a CPAP battery is simple once you have the average watts. Take the battery's rated watt-hours, multiply by an efficiency factor, and divide by the CPAP's average draw. Use roughly 0.85 if you are running through the AC inverter and roughly 0.9 if you are running a DC cable from the 12V port. That gives you hours of runtime. Divide by the hours you sleep to get nights.
          </p>

          <p>
            A worked example: a 288Wh station on DC, powering a machine that averages 25 watts with the humidifier off, gives 288 times 0.9, or about 259 usable watt-hours, divided by 25 watts, or about 10 hours. That is one full night with a little margin. Switch to AC with the humidifier on at 60 watts and the same battery gives 288 times 0.85, or about 245 watt-hours, divided by 60, which is about 4 hours. That is the same battery, two completely different answers.
          </p>

