          </div>

          <p>
            Dedicated CPAP packs like the Pilot-24 Lite are typically marketed with longer per-night figures than this table suggests, because battery makers assume low pressure settings and no humidification, and some machines draw well under 25 watts in those conditions. That is a reasonable claim for those conditions; just do not assume it applies to you until you have measured your own draw. The table also ignores the cold: lithium batteries deliver less capacity in a freezing tent, so add margin for winter camping.
          </p>

          <p>
            A useful rule: buy a battery with at least 1.5 times the capacity your math says you need for the nights you want to cover. Batteries lose a little capacity with age, your mask may leak more one night than another, and the cost of running out is waking up without therapy. If the math says one night on a 288Wh station, treat that station as a one-night battery, not a two-night battery, and plan a recharge between nights.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="airline" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            6. Flying With a CPAP Battery: The 100Wh and 160Wh Rules
          </h2>

          <p>
            Air travel is where dedicated CPAP batteries earn their price. Under FAA and common airline rules for passengers, spare lithium-ion batteries must go in carry-on bags, never checked luggage. Batteries rated at 100 watt-hours or less are generally allowed without airline approval. Batteries between 100 and 160 watt-hours need airline approval and are usually limited to two spares per passenger. Lithium batteries over 160 watt-hours are not permitted on passenger aircraft at all.
          </p>

          <p>
            That last line rules out every portable power station on this list. A 286Wh River 3 Plus or 288Wh Explorer 300 Plus cannot fly, regardless of the fact that you need it for a medical device. This is why the Pilot-24 Lite exists: it is rated just under 100Wh specifically so that it falls into the simplest category. Check that the watt-hour rating is printed on the battery itself, because security screeners may ask, and an unlabeled pack can be refused.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#38BDF8] mb-3">BEFORE YOU FLY</p>
            <p class="text-xl font-bold text-white mb-3">Check your airline's own battery and medical-device policy, not just the FAA rule.</p>
            <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed m-0">Airlines can be stricter than the baseline, some require advance notice for using a CPAP onboard, and international carriers follow their own regulators. Carry a copy of your machine's manual page on battery use and a note from your doctor.</p>
          </div>

          <p>
            If you intend to use the CPAP in flight, which some long-haul travelers do, the airline typically requires that the battery can run the device for longer than the flight duration with a margin, and some want advance notice through their medical or special-assistance desk. Using a CPAP onboard is usually done with humidification off. Airlines also commonly look for a manufacturer label stating the machine meets FAA requirements for in-flight use; most mainstream CPAPs carry one, but verify for your model before the trip.
          </p>

          <p>
            For a two-night trip with no reliable power, two sub-100Wh packs are usually the cleanest solution: both remain in the no-approval category, each can cover much or all of a night at low pressure with humidification off, and you can recharge both at the destination. A travel CPAP such as the ResMed AirMini is designed with this kind of trip in mind and pairs with compact batteries, but whether a travel machine suits your prescription is a question for your doctor, not a battery guide.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="camping" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            7. Camping With a CPAP: A Portable Setup That Works
          </h2>

          <p>
            A portable CPAP setup for camping is mostly a capacity-and-recharge plan. The battery has to cover each night, and something has to put energy back during the day. For a weekend, a fully charged 288Wh station with a DC cable and the humidifier off covers the first night and much of the second. For anything longer, you need a way to recharge: a folding solar panel, a car's 12V outlet while driving, or a campground hookup.
          </p>

          <p>
            Solar is the natural partner. A 100-watt folding panel aimed well on a clear summer day can put back very roughly 300 to 500 watt-hours, which refills a 288Wh station; on an overcast day, plan on a fraction of that. Our guides to the <a href="/best-portable-power-station-for-camping/">best power stations for camping</a> and <a href="/best-portable-solar-panel/">best portable solar panels</a> cover pairings, and the <a href="/best-solar-generator/">solar generator guide</a> walks through the sun-hours math in detail. Keep the battery in the tent or vehicle overnight, not outside in the cold, and move it into the shade during the day.
          </p>

          <p>
            Drive-in campers can also charge from the car. Most stations accept 12V car charging, though at a modest rate, so a few hours of driving puts back a partial charge rather than a full one. Never idle a vehicle near a tent to charge a battery: exhaust carbon monoxide is just as dangerous from a car as from a generator. If you are at a site with a generator, our guide to the <a href="/best-generator-for-camping/">best generators for camping</a> covers quiet models, but follow the same rule as at home: let the generator charge the battery during the day and let the battery run the CPAP overnight.
          </p>

          <p>
            Pack a backup. That means the DC cable and the CPAP's own AC brick, distilled water if you use a humidifier, and a plan for a night if the battery comes up short. Many experienced campers bring a second small battery, and for backpacking, where every ounce counts, a single sub-100Wh dedicated pack and a humidifier-off night is the realistic ceiling. Test the full setup at home on battery for a night before you leave, so the first failure happens in your bedroom and not at a trailhead.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="ups-mode" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            8. UPS Mode: A Bedside Backup That Never Wakes You
          </h2>

          <p>
            At home, the most valuable thing a power station can do for a CPAP user is act as an uninterruptible power supply. Plug the station into the wall, plug the CPAP into the station, and the station passes grid power through to the machine. When the grid fails, the station switches to its battery fast enough that the CPAP should keep running. Without that, an outage at night stops therapy and many machines will not restart until you notice and press a button.
          </p>

          <p>
            Switchover speed is the spec to look for. EcoFlow rates the River 3 Plus and Delta 3 Plus EPS modes at 10 milliseconds or less, which is within the range most electronics tolerate. Not every power station offers a true UPS function, and some that offer pass-through charging have a noticeable gap during switchover, so check the current spec sheet for a stated switchover time before you buy for this purpose. A traditional computer UPS also works for this role, and our guide to the <a href="/best-ups-battery-backup/">best UPS battery backups</a> covers those, but most office UPS units are designed for a few minutes of runtime, not a whole night.
          </p>

          <p>
            Leaving a station permanently plugged in raises reasonable questions about battery wear. LFP chemistry, which every station on this list uses, tolerates being held at a high state of charge much better than older lithium chemistries, and several brands let you set a charge limit in the app. Setting the limit to around 80 to 90 percent for a bedside UPS is a sensible compromise if you do not expect long outages, though it trims your runtime accordingly.
          </p>

          <p>
            Noise is the other bedside consideration. A station charging fast spins up its cooling fans, and a fan cycling on at 2 a.m. can be more disruptive than the CPAP. Most of the time, a station in pass-through mode with a full battery is quiet, but after an outage it will recharge when the grid returns. If your station lets you set a slower AC charge rate, use it for a bedroom unit.
          </p>
        </div>

