        <div id="which-to-buy" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            10. Which CPAP Battery to Buy
          </h2>

          <p>
            If you want one battery that handles a power outage at home and a weekend camping trip, buy the EcoFlow River 3 Plus. It is the right capacity for a night with a DC cable, its fast EPS mode makes it a real bedside backup, and the optional extra battery lets it grow into a multi-night unit later. The Jackery Explorer 300 Plus is the simpler alternative if you do not need UPS function and value a light, uncomplicated box.
          </p>

          <p>
            If you fly, buy the Medistrom Pilot-24 Lite, or the Pilot-12 Lite if your machine is a 12V model, and confirm the cable for your exact CPAP. For a two-night trip, buy two. If your CPAP travels with a laptop, phone and tablet that all need charging, the Anker SOLIX C300 makes the most of a small station's USB-C output.
          </p>

          <p>
            If you live where outages last days, step up to a kilowatt-hour station. Pick the EcoFlow Delta 3 Plus if a seamless bedside UPS is the priority and you may expand it later; pick the Jackery Explorer 1000 v2 if you want the lightest 1kWh box. Either will also run a refrigerator during the day, and our guides to the <a href="/best-home-battery-backup/">best home battery backup</a> and <a href="/best-portable-power-station-for-home-backup/">power stations for home backup</a> cover bigger systems if you want whole-room coverage.
          </p>

          <p>
            And if you already own a generator or are shopping for the best generator for a CPAP machine, buy a quiet inverter model like the Honda EU2200i and use it to recharge a battery during the day, not to run the CPAP overnight. Our <a href="/best-home-generator-for-power-outages/">home generator guide</a> covers larger units for whole-house needs. Whatever you buy, test it on a normal night before you need it, and confirm the DC cable and settings with your CPAP manufacturer or DME supplier.
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- Common Mistakes -->
        <div id="mistakes" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            11. Common Mistakes to Avoid
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6 my-8">
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Sizing the battery from the power brick label</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                The 90-watt rating on many CPAP power supplies is the maximum, not the average. Sizing from it overestimates draw and can push you toward a heavier battery than you need. Measure real overnight use with a plug-in watt meter, once with the humidifier on and once with it off.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Forgetting the humidifier doubles the draw</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Heated humidification and heated tubing can turn a 25-watt machine into a 60-watt one, especially in a cold room. A battery that covers two humidifier-free nights may not last one with everything on. Decide which settings you will use on battery nights first, ideally after measuring both, then buy a battery sized for them.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Buying the wrong voltage cable</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                ResMed AirSense machines run on 24V, other brands on 12V, and battery makers sell different packs and cables for each. A 12V pack with a 24V machine will not work. Check your exact model on the battery maker's compatibility chart, or ask your DME supplier, before ordering.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Trying to fly with a power station</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Lithium batteries over 160 watt-hours are not allowed on passenger aircraft, and stations in the 286-288Wh class exceed that. Medical need does not change the rule. Pack a sub-100Wh dedicated CPAP battery in your carry-on for flights, confirm your airline's policy in advance, and leave the power station at home for outages and drive-in camping.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Running a generator indoors or near a tent</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                A generator, or an idling car, produces carbon monoxide that can kill a sleeping person. Never run either in a garage, tent or near open windows. Run the generator outdoors by day to recharge a battery, and let the battery power the CPAP silently overnight.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <PlanTheJob />

        <!-- FAQ -->
        <div id="faq" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            12. Frequently Asked Questions
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6">
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What is the best battery backup for a CPAP?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                For most people, a roughly 300 watt-hour LFP power station like the EcoFlow River 3 Plus, used with a DC cable, is the best all-round choice. It covers about a night with the humidifier off, works as a bedside backup, and doubles as a camping battery. Frequent flyers should add a sub-100Wh dedicated pack like the Medistrom Pilot-24 Lite.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How long will a battery run a CPAP?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Divide the battery's watt-hours, times about 0.9 on DC or 0.85 on AC, by your machine's average watts. A 288Wh station on DC with a 25-watt draw gives roughly 10 hours. Turn on a heated humidifier and the same battery may give only about 4 hours. Measure your machine's real draw with a watt meter for an accurate answer.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Can I run my CPAP from a power station's AC outlet?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Yes. Every station here has a pure sine wave inverter that can run a CPAP's standard power brick. The trade-off is efficiency: the inverter wastes energy, so you get less runtime than with a DC converter from the 12V port. Use AC if your machine needs it for heated humidification, and confirm the setup with your CPAP manufacturer.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What size CPAP battery can I take on a plane?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Under FAA rules for passengers, spare lithium batteries go in carry-on only. Batteries of 100Wh or less are generally allowed without approval; 100 to 160Wh need airline approval and are usually limited to two; anything over 160Wh is prohibited. Check your airline's own medical-device policy too, since carriers can be stricter.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What is the best generator for a CPAP machine?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                A quiet inverter generator such as the Honda EU2200i, used outdoors to recharge a power station during the day. Inverter output suits sensitive electronics. Running a generator all night just for a CPAP wastes fuel, disturbs neighbors and puts a carbon monoxide source near sleeping people, so let a battery run the machine overnight.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Will a CPAP restart by itself after a power outage?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                It depends on the machine and settings. Some CPAPs resume therapy when power returns, others wait for you to press start, and many users only discover the answer during an outage. A power station with a fast UPS or EPS mode avoids the question by keeping the machine running through the outage. Check your manual or ask your DME supplier.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Is a power station a medical device?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                No. The power stations in this guide are general-purpose consumer batteries and are not medically certified. They supply power to your CPAP the same way a wall outlet does. Your CPAP manufacturer lists approved power accessories, and your doctor and DME supplier should confirm any battery setup before you rely on it for therapy.
              </p>
            </div>

          </div>
        </div>

